,"gender":"M","born":"1932-02-10","died":"2017-11-23","birthplace":"Colihaut","parties":["Dominica Labour Party"],"education":["University of the West Indies"],"occupations":["diplomat","politician"],"wiki":"Osborne Riviere"},"Q57823":{"id":"Q57823","name":"Roosevelt Skerrit","desc":"Prime Minister of Dominica","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/RooseveltSkerrit.jpg/330px-RooseveltSkerrit.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1972-06-08","died":null,"birthplace":"Roseau","parties":["Dominica Labour Party"],"education":["New Mexico State University","University of Mississippi","Dominica Grammar School"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Roosevelt Skerrit"},"Q1707507":{"id":"Q1707507","name":"Eliud Williams","desc":"President of Dominica","img":null,"gender":"M","born":"1948-08-21","died":null,"birthplace":"Roseau","parties":["Dominica Labour Party"],"education":["University of the West Indies"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Eliud Williams"},"Q2960197":{"id":"Q2960197","name":"Charles Savarin","desc":"President of Dominica (2013–2023)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Savarin_entering_(cropped).jpg/330px-Savarin_entering_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1943-10-02","died":null,"birthplace":"Portsmouth","parties":["Dominica Labour Party"],"education":["Ruskin College"],"occupations":["diplomat","politician"],"wiki":"Charles Savarin"},"Q122208779":{"id":"Q122208779","name":"Sylvanie Burton","desc":"Dominican politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Sylvanie_Burton_(cropped).jpg/330px-Sylvanie_Burton_(cropped).jpg","gender":"F","born":"1956-00-00","died":null,"birthplace":"Salybia","parties":["Dominica Labour Party"],"education":["St. Francis Xavier University","University of Manchester"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Sylvanie Burton"}},"summary":"Dominica officially the Commonwealth of Dominica, is an island country in the Caribbean. It is part of the Windward Islands chain in the Lesser Antilles archipelago in the Caribbean Sea. The capital, Roseau, is located on the western side of the island. Dominica's closest neighbours are two constituent territories of the European Union, both overseas departments of France: Guadeloupe to the northwest and Martinique to the south-southeast. Dominica comprises a land area of 750 km2 (290 sq mi), and the highest point is Morne Diablotins, at 1,448 m (4,751 ft) in elevation. The population was 71,293 at the 2011 census.\nThe island was settled by the Arawak arriving from South America in the fifth century. The Kalinago displaced the Arawak by the 15th century. Christopher Columbus is said to have passed the island on Sunday, 3rd November 1493. It was later colonised by Europeans, predominantly by the French from  1690 to 1763. The French enslaved West Africans and transported them to Dominica to work on coffee plantations. Great Britain took possession in 1763 after the Seven Years' War and continued the legacy of forced labor to gain wealth, and gradually established English as its official language. The island gained independence as a republic in 1978.\nDominica has been nicknamed the \"Nature Island of the Caribbean\" for its natural environment. It is the youngest island in the Lesser Antilles, and is still being formed by Geothermal-Volcanic activity, as evidenced by the world's second-largest hot spring, called Boiling Lake. The island has lush mountainous rainforests and is the home of many rare plants, animals, and bird species. There are dry shrubland areas in some of the western coastal regions, but heavy rainfall occurs inland. The Sisserou Parrot, also known as the imperial amazon, is critically endangered and found only on Dominica. It is the island's national bird and is featured on the National flag, making Dominica one of the two sovereign nations whose official flag features the color purple. The country is a member of the Commonwealth of Nations, the United Nations, the Organization of American States, the Organisation internationale de la Francophonie, the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States and the Non-Aligned Movement.\n\n","history":"The first written records in the history of Dominica began in November 1493, when Christopher Columbus spotted the island. Prior to European contact, Dominica was inhabited by the Arawak. Dominica was a French colony from 1715 until the end of the Seven Years' War in 1763, and then became a British colony from 1763 to 1978. It became an independent nation in 1978.","historyTitle":"History of Dominica","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,59372],[1961,60209],[1962,60948],[1963,61739],[1964,62587],[1965,63505],[1966,64507],[1967,65585],[1968,66732],[1969,67934],[1970,68908],[1971,69532],[1972,70060],[1973,70585],[1974,71091],[1975,71570],[1976,72017],[1977,72406],[1978,72724],[1979,72959],[1980,73083],[1981,72986],[1982,72688],[1983,72365],[1984,72056],[1985,71744],[1986,71406],[1987,71023],[1988,70608],[1989,70153],[1990,69627],[1991,69296],[1992,69325],[1993,69433],[1994,69477],[1995,69469],[1996,69410],[1997,69299],[1998,69124],[1999,68870],[2000,68511],[2001,68288],[2002,68367],[2003,68531],[2004,68649],[2005,68734],[2006,68806],[2007,68847],[2008,68854],[2009,68864],[2010,68859],[2011,69053],[2012,69118],[2013,69715],[2014,70373],[2015,70410],[2016,69606],[2017,68496],[2018,68174],[2019,67880],[2020,67573],[2021,67202],[2022,66826],[2023,66510],[2024,66205],[2025,65871]],"gdpPerCapita":[[1977,633.552],[1978,785.576],[1979,754.091],[1980,996.191],[1981,1124.97],[1982,1231.67],[1983,1363.44],[1984,1514.89],[1985,1665.53],[1986,1892.87],[1987,2138.3],[1988,2423.33],[1989,2639.05],[1990,2892.98],[1991,3171.37],[1992,3376.26],[1993,3536.16],[1994,3805.2],[1995,3951.72],[1996,4211],[1997,4372.2],[1998,4664.24],[1999,4817.18],[2000,4867.4],[2001,5527.52],[2002,5488.03],[2003,5447.44],[2004,5778.83],[2005,5695.22],[2006,6055.78],[2007,6459.04],[2008,6971.33],[2009,7487.49],[2010,7549.62]